drm: let a disconnected output be disabled

CDRMOutput::commitState refuses every commit once the connector reports
disconnected, including a commit whose only purpose is to turn the output off.
A compositor disables an output when it goes away -- Hyprland does it from
CMonitor::onDisconnect -- and that disable is rejected with "Cannot commit a
disconnected output".

Nothing else brings the hardware down. recheckCRTCs drops our own crtc
assignment and logs "is not connected, clearing stale crtc N", but that is
bookkeeping, not an atomic commit: the CRTC stays active with the mode it was
last driving. The next connect creates a fresh output and modesets onto a CRTC
that was never torn down.

Observed on an NVIDIA-driven DP sink that powers its input down when the
monitor is switched to another source. The connector bounces, the reconnect
modeset reports success, and the panel stays dark. The only cure is a manual
DPMS off/on, which works for exactly one reason: by then the connector is
connected again, so the same guard lets the disable through.

Split the two cases the guard was conflating. A commit from a stale output
object is still refused unconditionally. A commit on a disconnected connector
is refused only when it would leave the output enabled; one that disables it
proceeds. The crtc is still attached at this point -- recheckCRTCs clears it
later -- so the disable has somewhere to land, and every buffer, format and
VRR path downstream is already gated on the enabled state.
---
--- a/src/backend/drm/DRM.cpp
+++ b/src/backend/drm/DRM.cpp
@@ -2494,7 +2494,17 @@
         return false;
     }
 
-    if (connector->status != DRM_MODE_CONNECTED || connector->output != self.lock()) {
+    // A commit from a stale output object is always wrong. A disconnected connector,
+    // though, must still be able to be turned OFF. The compositor disables an output
+    // when it goes away, and refusing that commit leaves the CRTC active with the mode
+    // it was last driving: recheckCRTCs only drops our own crtc assignment, which is
+    // bookkeeping, not a commit, so nothing ever brings the hardware down. The next
+    // connect then modesets onto a CRTC that was never torn down, and on some sinks the
+    // picture does not come back until a disable is committed by hand.
+    //
+    // The crtc is still attached at this point -- it is cleared later, in recheckCRTCs --
+    // so the disable has somewhere to land.
+    if (connector->output != self.lock() || (connector->status != DRM_MODE_CONNECTED && state->state().enabled)) {
         backend->backend->log(AQ_LOG_ERROR, "drm: Cannot commit a disconnected output");
         return false;
     }
